# Genre Analysis Among Winners

## Problem Description

The goal of the analysis is to examine whether there is a statistically significant relationship between a film's genre and the probability of winning an Oscar. Due to the multiplicity of genres (one film can belong to multiple genres), we conduct the analysis in two ways:

- **Single-label approach:** We assign one main genre to each film (`Main.Genre`)
- **Multi-label approach:** A film can belong to multiple genres which are counted separately (`Movie.Genre`)

We test both approaches with the chi-square test of independence at significance level $\alpha = 0.05$ with hypotheses:

- **H0:** There is no significant difference in the frequency of wins among different film genres
- **H1:** There is a significant difference in the frequency of wins among different film genres

## Single-label Analysis

### Data Preparation for Single-label Analysis

```{r}
winners <- oscars %>% filter(Award == "Winner")
nominees <- oscars %>% filter(Award %in% c("Nominee", "Winner"))

genre_summary <- nominees %>%
  count(Main.Genre, name = "Nominations") %>%
  left_join(winners %>% count(Main.Genre, name = "Winners"), by = "Main.Genre") %>%
  mutate(
    Winners = replace_na(Winners, 0),
    NotWinner = Nominations - Winners,
    Win_Rate = Winners / Nominations * 100
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(Win_Rate))

kable(genre_summary[, c("Main.Genre", "Nominations", "Winners", "Win_Rate")], 
      col.names = c("Genre", "Nominations", "Wins", "Win Rate (%)"),
      digits = 1,
      caption = "Distribution of nominations and wins by genre (≥5 nominations)")
```

From the table, it is evident that the Drama genre has the most nominations (215) and wins (42), but in win rate it is equal to the Adventure genre (~20%). We excluded genres with fewer than 5 nominations for statistical reliability.

### Distribution Visualization  

Due to large differences in the number of occurrences among genres, logarithmic transformation of the data was applied.

```{r}
genre_plot_data <- genre_summary %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = c(Winners, NotWinner),
    names_to = "Outcome",
    values_to = "Count"
  )

ggplot(genre_plot_data, aes(x = reorder(Main.Genre, Nominations), 
                             y = Count + 1, fill = Outcome)) +
  geom_col(color = "black") +
  scale_y_log10(breaks = c(1, 10, 100)) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c("Winners" = "#66c2a5", "NotWinner" = "#5e31ac"),
    labels = c("Others", "Winners")
  ) +
  labs(x = "Genre", y = "Number of films (log10)", fill = "") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 11) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

Drama dominates in absolute number of films, while the relative ratios of winners and others remain fairly balanced; the graph is polarized because some genres have almost equal ratios, while other bars have no winners at all. 

### Chi-square Test

```{r}
common_genres <- c("Biography", "Comedy", "Drama")
oscars$GenreGroup <- ifelse(oscars$Main.Genre %in% common_genres,
                             as.character(oscars$Main.Genre), "Other")

winner_counts <- as.data.frame(table(subset(oscars, Award == "Winner")$GenreGroup))
colnames(winner_counts) <- c("GenreGroup", "Winners")

nominee_counts <- as.data.frame(table(subset(oscars, Award %in% c("Winner", "Nominee"))$GenreGroup))
colnames(nominee_counts) <- c("GenreGroup", "Nominations")

df <- merge(nominee_counts, winner_counts, by = "GenreGroup", all.x = TRUE)
df$Winners[is.na(df$Winners)] <- 0
df$NotWinner <- df$Nominations - df$Winners

kontigencijska_single <- as.data.frame(df[, c("Winners", "NotWinner")])
row.names(kontigencijska_single) <- df$GenreGroup

kable(kontigencijska_single, caption = "Contingency table: Winners vs Others by genre")

```

We prepared a contingency table for the chi-square test. We group rare genres (with fewer than 10 films) into the "Other" category.
We conduct a chi-square test of independence to check whether there are significant differences in the frequency of wins among genres.

```{r}
chisq_single <- chisq.test(kontigencijska_single)
chisq_single
```

### Results

Given that $p = 0.3011 > 0.05$, we accept H0. There is no statistically significant relationship between genre and winning an Oscar in the single-label approach.

Standardized residuals show how genres deviate from expected values:

```{r}
kable(round(chisq_single$stdres, 2), 
      caption = "Standardized residuals (values > ±2 are significant)")
```

No genre shows extreme deviations (all residuals are between -2 and +2), which confirms the conclusion about the absence of significant relationship.

## Multi-label Analysis 

### Data Preparation 

For multi-label analysis, we separate films with multiple genres into separate rows. Each genre is counted separately, allowing better understanding of complex genre combinations.  
It should also be noted that in multi-label analysis, the independence assumption may be slightly violated because one film belongs to multiple genres, so the p-value of the chi-square test should be interpreted with caution.

```{r}
oscars_long <- oscars %>%
  separate_rows(Movie.Genre, sep = ",") %>%
  mutate(Movie.Genre = trimws(Movie.Genre))
```

Each film can now contribute to multiple genre categories (e.g., "Drama, Romance" will be counted in both Drama and Romance).

Calculation of the number of wins and non-wins by genre:

```{r}
genre_counts <- oscars_long %>%
  mutate(Outcome = ifelse(Award == "Winner", "Winner", "NotWinner")) %>%
  count(Movie.Genre, Outcome) %>%
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = Outcome,
    values_from = n,
    values_fill = 0
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    Total = Winner + NotWinner,
    GenreGroup = ifelse(Total < 5, "Other", Movie.Genre),
    SuccessRate = Winner / Total
  )
```

We group rare genres (with fewer than 5 occurrences) into the "Other" category to satisfy the chi-square test conditions (expected frequencies > 5).

```{r}
genre_table_ml <- genre_counts %>%
  group_by(GenreGroup) %>%
  summarise(
    Winners = sum(Winner),
    NotWinner = sum(NotWinner),
    Total = sum(Total),
    SuccessRate = sum(Winner) / sum(Total),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(Total))

kable(genre_table_ml, 
      digits = 3,
      col.names = c("Genre", "Wins", "Others", "Total", "Win Rate"),
      caption = "Multi-label genre distribution")
```
\newpage

### Visualization of Multi-label Genre Analysis Results
```{r}
genre_melt <- genre_table_ml %>%
  select(GenreGroup, Winners, NotWinner, Total) %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = c(Winners, NotWinner),
    names_to = "Outcome",
    values_to = "Count"
  )

ggplot(genre_melt, aes(x = reorder(GenreGroup, Total), 
                       y = Count + 1, fill = Outcome)) + 
  geom_col(color = "black") +
  scale_y_log10(breaks = c(1, 10, 100, 1000)) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Winners" = "#66c2a5", "NotWinner" = "#5e31ac"),
                    labels = c("Others", "Winners")) +
  labs(x = "Genre", y = "Number of occurrences (log10)", fill = "") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 11) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))

```

The graph shows that Drama, Romance, and Biography dominate in number of occurrences, reflecting their frequency in genre combinations.

### Chi-square Test

We use the contingency table for the multi-label approach and conduct a chi-square test of independence.

```{r}
kontigencijska_multi <- as.matrix(genre_table_ml[, c("Winners", "NotWinner")])
row.names(kontigencijska_multi) <- genre_table_ml$GenreGroup

chisq_multi <- chisq.test(kontigencijska_multi)
chisq_multi
```

The result obtained is $p = 0.1036 > 0.05$, which means we accept H0. Even in the multi-label approach, there is no statistically significant relationship between genre and the probability of winning an Oscar.

Standardized residuals:

```{r}
kable(round(chisq_multi$stdres, 2), 
      caption = "Standardized residuals (multi-label)")
```

## Conclusion

Based on the results obtained from the Chi-square test in both approaches, we accept H0 and can conclude that there are no significant differences in the frequency of wins among different genres of films nominated for an Oscar. Standardized residuals indicate which genres significantly deviate from expected values, which can be useful for further research and understanding trends in award distribution, as well as the question of why some genres are more frequently nominated than others.

