# Analysis of Differences in Average Film Duration Among Genres

## Problem Description  
The main question of the next analysis is to determine whether there are statistically significant differences in the average duration of films of different genres. To investigate this, we will examine the distribution of film duration by main genres (Main.Genre) and apply appropriate statistical tests. First, let's review how many films belong to each genre in our data.  

## Data Preparation  
```{r}
count <- table(oscars$Main.Genre)
count_df <- as.data.frame(count)
colnames(count_df) <- c("Main Genre", "Number of Films")
kable(count_df, caption = "Number of films by main genre")
```
Due to the small number of films in some genres, we will classify those films into the 'Other' category so that the analysis is reliable and the results clear for interpretation.

```{r}
oscars$Main.Genre2 <- as.character(oscars$Main.Genre)
small_genres <- c("Animation","Family","Film-Noir","Horror",
                  "Music","Musical","Romance","Western")
oscars$Main.Genre2[oscars$Main.Genre2 %in% small_genres] <- "Other"
oscars$Main.Genre2 <- factor(oscars$Main.Genre2)
paste0("Number of films with 'Other' genre: ", sum(oscars$Main.Genre2=="Other"))
```
Until the end of this analysis, we will use the Main.Genre2 column as the genre.  

## ANOVA Assumptions
We will best determine whether there are differences in average film duration among genres by applying one-way ANOVA or its non-parametric alternative, the Kruskal-Wallis test. We choose the appropriate test depending on whether the following assumptions are satisfied:  
  
1 - Normality of film duration data distribution  
2 - Homogeneity of variances of film duration data   
3 - Independence of observations   

Let's now check data normality by genre using the Lilliefors test at significance level $\alpha = 0.05$. The hypotheses are:  
**H0**: Data within the genre follow a normal distribution  
**H1**: Data within the genre do not follow a normal distribution.
```{r}
razliciti_genres <- levels(oscars$Main.Genre2)
normality_results <- data.frame(Genre = character(), 
                                P_value = numeric(), 
                                stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
for (genre in razliciti_genres) {
  genre_data <- oscars$Movie.Time[oscars$Main.Genre2 == genre]
  genre_test <- lillie.test(genre_data)

  normality_results <- rbind(normality_results, 
                             data.frame(Genre = genre, 
                                        P_value = genre_test$p.value))
}

kable(normality_results, 
      col.names = c("Genre", "P-value"), 
      caption = "Lilliefors normality test of film duration by genre")
```
Since most distributions of film duration by genre are not normal (p-value < 0.05), we will not apply the Bartlett test or one-way ANOVA, but use the (less statistically powerful) Kruskal-Wallis test.

## Kruskal-Wallis Test
Let's now conduct the test at significance level $\alpha = 0.05$ with the following hypotheses:  
  
**H0**: The medians of all samples are equal  
**H1**: At least two medians are not equal.   
  
The condition for test applicability is satisfied, that is, the size of each sample is at least 5.


```{r}
kruskal.test(Movie.Time ~ Main.Genre2, data = oscars)
```
The Kruskal-Wallis test results show a very small p-value, which means we reject the null hypothesis in favor of the alternative hypothesis. At least one distribution of duration differs significantly from the others, that is, there is a difference in population medians.   

## Visual and Descriptive Analysis
It remains for us to visually determine which genres are characterized by longer or shorter film durations, which we can show using box plots by genre.
```{r}
boxplot(Movie.Time ~ Main.Genre2,
        data = oscars,
        main = "Film duration by main genre",
        xlab = "",
        ylab = "Duration (minutes)",
        las = 2)
```
Let's also look at the mean values:  
```{r}
mean_table <- oscars %>%
  group_by(Main.Genre2) %>%
  summarise(Mean_Duration = mean(Movie.Time, na.rm = TRUE),
            Count = n()) %>%
  arrange(desc(Mean_Duration))
colnames(mean_table) <- c("Main Genre", "Average Film Duration", "Number of Films")
kable(mean_table, caption = "Average film duration by main genre")
```
"Adventure" genre films stand out as the longest on average.
