# Can We Predict How a Film Is Rated?

## Problem Description

We investigate whether film characteristics (year of release, duration, genre, content rating) can statistically significantly explain IMDb rating using a multiple linear regression model. The goal is to build a model that will enable understanding of which characteristics affect the rating and how strong that influence is.

## Descriptive Statistics

We begin with an analysis of basic descriptive measures for the numerical variables we use in the model.

```{r}
desc_stats <- oscars %>%
  summarise(
    across(c(IMDB.Rating, Year.of.Release, Movie.Time),
           list(Mean = ~mean(., na.rm = TRUE),
                Median = ~median(., na.rm = TRUE),
                SD = ~sd(., na.rm = TRUE)))
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = "Variable", values_to = "Value") %>%
  separate(Variable, into = c("Variable", "Stat"), sep = "_(?=[^_]+$)") %>%
  pivot_wider(names_from = Stat, values_from = Value)

kable(desc_stats, 
      col.names = c("Variable", "Mean", "Median", "Std. dev."),
      digits = 2,
      caption = "Descriptive statistics of key variables")

```

The average IMDb rating of nominated films is 7.6 (SD = 0.56), indicating relatively high quality and low variability. The average year of release is 1973, reflecting the data range from 1927 to 2020.

### Correlation Matrix

We calculate the correlation matrix to examine linear relationships between our numerical variables.

```{r}
cor_matrix <- cor(oscars[, c("IMDB.Rating", "Movie.Time", "Year.of.Release")], 
                  use = "complete.obs")

kable(round(cor_matrix, 3),
      caption = "Pearson correlation matrix")
```

We observe a moderate positive correlation between year of release and rating ($r = 0.330$), and a weak positive correlation between duration and rating ($r = 0.292$). Newer and longer films tend toward higher ratings.

## Multiple Linear Regression Model

### Dummy Variable Preparation

We convert categorical variables (genre, content rating) into dummy variables so we can include them in the linear model.

```{r}
oscars_model <- dummy_cols(oscars, 
                            select_columns = c("Main.Genre", "Content.Rating"),
                            remove_first_dummy = TRUE,
                            remove_selected_columns = FALSE)

names(oscars_model) <- make.names(names(oscars_model))
genre_cols <- grep("^Main.Genre_", names(oscars_model), value = TRUE)
rating_cols <- grep("^Content.Rating_", names(oscars_model), value = TRUE)

all_predictors <- c("Year.of.Release", "Movie.Time", genre_cols, rating_cols)
```

Dummy variables allow inclusion of categorical variables in the model so that each category gets its own indicator variable. It is important to omit one dummy variable from each categorical variable to avoid multicollinearity.

### Model Estimation

We estimate a multiple linear regression model with IMDb rating as the dependent variable.

```{r}
model_formula <- reformulate(termlabels = all_predictors, response = "IMDB.Rating")
fit_multi <- lm(model_formula, data = oscars_model)

summary(fit_multi)
```

From the model summary, we see that Adjusted R² = `r round(summary(fit_multi)$adj.r.squared, 3)`, which means the model explains ~`r round(summary(fit_multi)$adj.r.squared * 100, 1)`% of the variance in IMDb ratings.

Film duration (Movie.Time) proves to be the most consistent predictor, while year of release loses significance after controlling for other factors.

### Model Diagnostics

We check the assumptions of linear regression using a diagnostic Q-Q plot.

```{r}
qqnorm(residuals(fit_multi),
       main = "Q–Q plot of residuals",
       xlab = "Theoretical quantiles",
       ylab = "Sample quantiles")
qqline(residuals(fit_multi))

```

The Q–Q plot shows that the residuals largely lie along the diagonal, suggesting an approximately normal distribution of residuals and satisfaction of the normality assumption.  
We additionally check residual normality with the Lilliefors test.

```{r}
lillie.test(rstandard(fit_multi))
```

The Lilliefors test confirms residual normality ($p > 0.05$), validating the assumptions of linear regression.


## Bootstrap Analysis

We use bootstrap to estimate the robustness of model coefficients without strict assumptions about data distribution. We conduct 1000 bootstrap iterations to estimate the distribution of coefficients and confidence intervals.

```{r}
set.seed(42)

formula_string <- paste("IMDB.Rating ~", paste(all_predictors, collapse = " + "))
n_expected_coefs <- length(all_predictors) + 1

bootstrap_lm <- function(data, indices) {
  d <- data[indices, ]
  tryCatch({
    fit <- lm(as.formula(formula_string), data = d)
    coefs <- coef(fit)
    if (length(coefs) == n_expected_coefs) {
      return(coefs)
    } else {
      return(rep(NA, n_expected_coefs))
    }
  }, error = function(e) {
    return(rep(NA, n_expected_coefs))
  })
}

boot_results <- boot::boot(data = oscars_model, 
                           statistic = bootstrap_lm,
                           R = 1000)
```

Bootstrap standard errors allow comparison with classical OLS standard errors. The similarity of these values confirms the robustness of the estimates.

### Bootstrap Distribution Visualization

We display bootstrap distributions for three key model coefficients: intercept, year of release, and film duration.

```{r, fig.width=14, fig.height=12}
par(mfrow = c(2, 2))
original_coefs <- coef(fit_multi)

hist(boot_results$t[, 1], breaks = 30,
     main = "Bootstrap: Intercept",
     xlab = "Coefficient", ylab = "Frequency", col = "yellow", border = "white")
abline(v = original_coefs[1], col = "red", lwd = 2)
abline(v = quantile(boot_results$t[, 1], c(0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE),
       col = "darkred", lwd = 2, lty = 2)

hist(boot_results$t[, 2], breaks = 30,
     main = "Bootstrap: Year.of.Release",
     xlab = "Coefficient", ylab = "Frequency", col = "lightblue", border = "white")
abline(v = original_coefs[2], col = "red", lwd = 2)
abline(v = quantile(boot_results$t[, 2], c(0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE),
       col = "darkred", lwd = 2, lty = 2)

hist(boot_results$t[, 3], breaks = 30,
     main = "Bootstrap: Movie.Time",
     xlab = "Coefficient", ylab = "Frequency", col = "lightgreen", border = "white")
abline(v = original_coefs[3], col = "red", lwd = 2)
abline(v = quantile(boot_results$t[, 3], c(0.025, 0.975), na.rm = TRUE),
       col = "darkred", lwd = 2, lty = 2)

par(mfrow = c(1, 1))
```

The red line indicates the estimate from the original model, while the dashed lines represent the 95% bootstrap confidence interval. The bootstrap distributions show the stability of coefficient estimates.

```{r}
adj_r2 <- summary(fit_multi)$adj.r.squared
coef_summary <- summary(fit_multi)$coefficients
significant_coefs <- sum(coef_summary[, 4] < 0.05, na.rm = TRUE)
total_coefs <- nrow(coef_summary)
```

## Conclusion

The multiple linear regression model uses year of film release, film duration, genre, and content rating to predict the IMDb rating of films nominated for an Oscar.

Adjusted R² is `r round(adj_r2, 4)`, which means the model explains `r round(adj_r2 * 100, 2)`% of the variance in IMDb ratings. The F-test shows that the model is statistically significant (p < 0.001), meaning the features used have predictive power.

`r significant_coefs` out of `r total_coefs` coefficients are statistically significant at the 5% significance level, indicating that most variables included in the model have a significant contribution to rating prediction.

Bootstrap analysis was used for robustness of estimates. Bootstrap confidence intervals confirm the stability of coefficient estimates and allow inference without strict assumptions about data normality.

Although the model explains a significant portion of variability, the remaining unexplained variance indicates that there are other factors (directing quality, acting, story) that affect ratings but are not included in this model.

