# Winner Prediction Analysis

In this analysis, we investigate whether there are statistically significant differences between films that won an Oscar ("Winner") and those that were only nominated ("Nominee"). We conduct the analysis based on available features: IMDb rating, genre, film duration, number of IMDb votes.

## Data Preparation  

Before analysis, we organize the dataset: we create a binary variable for winning and remove records with NA values. Genres that are not frequently represented were previously grouped in the Main.Genre2 column, so we will use it for further analysis. Data on the year of film release was excluded from further analysis because detailed examination of the dataset showed that the number of winners per year is almost constant (most often 1–2), while the number of nominated films differs significantly between years. Due to this inconsistency, the year variable does not carry stable information but primarily introduces additional noise into the logistic regression model.  
```{r}
oscars <- oscars %>%
  mutate(
    IsWinner = ifelse(Award == "Winner", 1, 0),
    # Genre grouping for model stability
    Main.Genre2 = ifelse(Main.Genre %in% c("Drama", "Comedy", "Biography", 
                                           "Adventure", "Action", "Crime"), 
                        Main.Genre, "Other"),
    Main.Genre2 = factor(Main.Genre2)
  ) %>%
  filter(complete.cases(IMDB.Rating, Main.Genre2, Movie.Time, IsWinner, IMDB.Votes))

```

## Construction of a Simpler Logistic Regression Model
First, we will try to build a simpler logistic regression model in which the dependent variable will be IsWinner (0 = Nominated, 1 = Winner), while the independent variables will be IMDb rating and average film duration (Movie.Time).  

Before building the model, let's review the distribution of the number of films by outcome and calculate the average values of the mentioned parameters to gain insight into the basic characteristics of the dataset.

```{r}
tablica <- oscars %>%
  group_by(IsWinner) %>%
  summarise(
    `Number of films` = n(),
    `Average IMDb rating` = mean(IMDB.Rating),
    `Average duration` = mean(Movie.Time)
  ) %>%
  rename(
    Winner = IsWinner
  )
kable(tablica)
```
The data shows that among 94 winners, the average IMDb rating and film duration is slightly higher than among 477 nominees, suggesting that these factors may have a positive influence on the probability of winning an Oscar. It is also noticeable that the data is significantly imbalanced - there are far fewer winners compared to nominees, which can make precise prediction by the model more difficult.  

The following graph shows the distribution of films by IMDb rating and duration, separating winners and nominees, so we can visually notice differences between outcomes.
```{r}
ggplot(oscars, aes(x = IMDB.Rating, y = Movie.Time, color = factor(IsWinner))) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6, size = 2) +
  labs(x = "IMDb rating", y = "Film duration (min)", color = "Outcome") +
  scale_color_brewer(palette = "Set1") +
  theme_bw()
```
From the graph, it is difficult to discern a clear trend that would predictively distinguish outcomes by itself.  


Let's now build a simple logistic regression model to estimate the probability of winning an Oscar based on IMDb rating and average film duration.

```{r}
logreg.mdl <- glm(IsWinner ~ IMDB.Rating + Movie.Time,
             data = oscars,
             family = binomial())
summary(logreg.mdl)

```
The model shows that both variables (IMDb rating and film duration) significantly affect the probability of winning an Oscar. Positive coefficients indicate that a higher rating or longer film increases the chance of winning. The model fits better than the null model, as shown by the reduced deviance (510.77 → 476.63).

Pseudo-R² is calculated as $$ R^2 = 1 - \frac{\text{model deviance}}{\text{null model deviance}}. $$ The value shows how much better our model explains the data compared to a model without predictors.

```{r}
Rsq <- 1 - logreg.mdl$deviance / logreg.mdl$null.deviance
paste0("Pseudo-R² for simple model: ", round(Rsq, 4))

```
The obtained result indicates that the model explains only about 6.7% of the variability in the outcome, showing that most factors affecting Oscar wins are not covered by this model.

## Construction of a More Complex Logistic Regression Model  

Let's create a more complex logistic regression model expanded with additional predictors: grouped film genre (Main.Genre2) and number of IMDb votes (IMDB.Votes). The goal is to improve the model's ability to distinguish winners from nominees by including more information about film characteristics.  

```{r}
logreg.mdl2 <- glm(IsWinner ~ IMDB.Rating + Movie.Time + Main.Genre2 + IMDB.Votes,
             data = oscars,
             family = binomial())
summary(logreg.mdl2)
```
Added predictors (genre and number of votes) show different influences on the probability of winning, with some genres suggesting greater, and some lesser chances of winning the award.

To assess the significance of model improvement by adding new predictors, we use the Likelihood Ratio Test (LRT). This test compares the deviance of two models: the null model (simpler) and the expanded model (with additional variables) at significance level $\alpha = 0.05$. The test hypotheses are:  
H0: Additional predictors are not significant  
H1: At least one additional predictor is significant.

```{r}
anova(logreg.mdl, logreg.mdl2, test = "LRT")
```
The results show that adding the Main.Genre2 and IMDB.Votes variables to the model statistically significantly improves its fit, that is, the new model has significantly lower deviance.  
Let's also calculate Pseudo-R² for the new model:
```{r}
Rsq2 <- 1 - logreg.mdl2$deviance / logreg.mdl2$null.deviance
paste0("Pseudo-R² for expanded model: ", round(Rsq2, 4))
```
It is still relatively low, but there is an increase in explainable variability compared to the simple model.

## Model Evaluation 
For evaluation of the classification performance of the more complex model, we can use a confusion matrix, which compares actual and predicted values. Based on it, we calculate key measures: accuracy, precision, recall, and specificity, which provide more detailed insight into the model's ability to correctly predict winners and nominees.

```{r}
yHat <- logreg.mdl2$fitted.values >= 0.5
tab <- table(oscars$IsWinner, yHat)
kable(tab)
```
The table shows that the model very rarely correctly predicts winners, but False Positive values also occur rarely. This explains why overall accuracy can be high, even though the model poorly recognizes actual winners. Let's calculate all measures:
```{r}
tocnost <- sum(diag(tab)) / sum(tab)
preciznost <- tab[2,2] / sum(tab[,2])
odziv <- tab[2,2] / sum(tab[2,])
specificnost <- tab[1,1] / sum(tab[,1])
metrics <- data.frame(
  `Accuracy` = round(tocnost,4),
  `Precision` = round(preciznost,4),
  `Recall` = round(odziv,4),
  `Specificity` = round(specificnost,4)
)
kable(metrics)
```

As we could have predicted from the first results table, the accuracy parameter is high, while the recall parameter defined as $\dfrac{TP}{TP+FN}$ is extremely low.
